      {"type":"mc","q":"Which observation distinguishes evidence from a misconception about Newton's Third Law force pairs?","choices":["The reaction force begins after the action force ends.","Third-law force pairs act on the same object and cancel.","The larger object exerts a force but receives none.","Two force sensors between interacting carts record equal magnitudes in opposite directions at the same time."],"answer":"Two force sensors between interacting carts record equal magnitudes in opposite directions at the same time.","explain":"Two force sensors between interacting carts record equal magnitudes in opposite directions at the same time. This matches : identify equal-magnitude, opposite-direction simultaneous force pairs from object interactions","audio":"Which observation distinguishes evidence from a misconception about Newton's Third Law force pairs?"},
      {"type":"mc","q":"Which evidence-based connection explains how the parts of Newton's Third Law force pairs relate?","choices":["Third-law force pairs act on the same object and cancel.","The reaction force begins after the action force ends.","The paired forces act on different objects, so they do not cancel as forces on one object.","The larger object exerts a force but receives none."],"answer":"The paired forces act on different objects, so they do not cancel as forces on one object.","explain":"The paired forces act on different objects, so they do not cancel as forces on one object. This matches : identify equal-magnitude, opposite-direction simultaneous force pairs from object interactions","audio":"Which evidence-based connection explains how the parts of Newton's Third Law force pairs relate?"},
      {"type":"mc","q":"True or false: “Interaction evidence supports simultaneous equal-and-opposite pairs even when the objects accelerate differently because their masses differ” correctly answers “Which conclusion about Newton's Third Law force pairs uses the evidence without adding an assumption.”","choices":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explain":"True. Interaction evidence supports simultaneous equal-and-opposite pairs even when the objects accelerate differently because their masses differ. This matches : identify equal-magnitude, opposite-direction simultaneous force pairs from object interactions","audio":"True or false: “Interaction evidence supports simultaneous equal-and-opposite pairs even when the objects accelerate differently because their masses differ” correctly answers “Which conclusion about Newton's Third Law force pairs uses the evidence without adding an assumption.”"}
