      {"type":"mc","q":"A water shortage leads to higher prices, use limits, and investment in conservation. Which inference remains most defensible after checking chronology and context?","choices":["Every economic system allocates resources in exactly the same way.","Natural resources automatically create broad prosperity.","Different policies can change behavior while distributing costs differently.","Higher exports prove every group became better off."],"answer":"Different policies can change behavior while distributing costs differently.","explain":"Different policies can change behavior while distributing costs differently. This inference connects the named evidence to its context while avoiding an unsupported absolute conclusion.","audio":"A water shortage leads to higher prices, use limits, and investment in conservation. Which inference remains most defensible after checking chronology and context?"},
      {"type":"mc","q":"Two countries have similar income averages but different health outcomes and income distributions. Which inference remains most defensible after checking chronology and context?","choices":["Scarcity has no effect on economic decisions.","Multiple indicators give a fuller picture than one national average.","Every economic system allocates resources in exactly the same way.","Natural resources automatically create broad prosperity."],"answer":"Multiple indicators give a fuller picture than one national average.","explain":"Multiple indicators give a fuller picture than one national average. This inference connects the named evidence to its context while avoiding an unsupported absolute conclusion.","audio":"Two countries have similar income averages but different health outcomes and income distributions. Which inference remains most defensible after checking chronology and context?"},
      {"type":"mc","q":"A mine raises exports and tax revenue but displaces villages and pollutes water. Which inference remains most defensible after checking chronology and context?","choices":["A responsible evaluation weighs economic gains against social and environmental costs.","Natural resources automatically create broad prosperity.","Scarcity has no effect on economic decisions.","One indicator completely measures human well-being."],"answer":"A responsible evaluation weighs economic gains against social and environmental costs.","explain":"A responsible evaluation weighs economic gains against social and environmental costs. This inference connects the named evidence to its context while avoiding an unsupported absolute conclusion.","audio":"A mine raises exports and tax revenue but displaces villages and pollutes water. Which inference remains most defensible after checking chronology and context?"},
      {"type":"mc","q":"Evidence A: Government plans set some prices while private firms make many other production decisions. Evidence B: A region has mineral resources but lacks roads, trained workers, and investment. Which thesis best evaluates both sources without overstating either one?","choices":["Evidence A proves “Every economic system allocates resources in exactly the same way.,” so Evidence B can be ignored.","Both sources prove that one event caused every later development, regardless of chronology or context.","Because the sources discuss related topics, neither source needs corroboration.","Evidence A supports “Economic systems differ in how they answer what to produce, how to produce, and for whom.” Evidence B supports “Land, labor, capital, and entrepreneurship shape production choices.” Together, the sources show related developments while preserving each source's context."],"answer":"Evidence A supports “Economic systems differ in how they answer what to produce, how to produce, and for whom.” Evidence B supports “Land, labor, capital, and entrepreneurship shape production choices.” Together, the sources show related developments while preserving each source's context.","explain":"Evidence A supports “Economic systems differ in how they answer what to produce, how to produce, and for whom.” Evidence B supports “Land, labor, capital, and entrepreneurship shape production choices.” Together, the sources show related developments while preserving each source's context. This response states what each source supports and avoids turning related evidence into proof of a single, universal cause.","audio":"Evidence A: Government plans set some prices while private firms make many other production decisions. Evidence B: A region has mineral resources but lacks roads, trained workers, and investment. Which thesis best evaluates both sources without overstating either one?"},
      {"type":"mc","q":"Evidence A: A region has mineral resources but lacks roads, trained workers, and investment. Evidence B: A water shortage leads to higher prices, use limits, and investment in conservation. Which thesis best evaluates both sources without overstating either one?","choices":["Both sources prove that one event caused every later development, regardless of chronology or context.","Because the sources discuss related topics, neither source needs corroboration.","Evidence A supports “Land, labor, capital, and entrepreneurship shape production choices.” Evidence B supports “Scarcity requires choices, while incentives affect how households, firms, and governments respond.” Together, the sources show related developments while preserving each source's context.","Evidence A proves “Natural resources automatically create broad prosperity.,” so Evidence B can be ignored."],"answer":"Evidence A supports “Land, labor, capital, and entrepreneurship shape production choices.” Evidence B supports “Scarcity requires choices, while incentives affect how households, firms, and governments respond.” Together, the sources show related developments while preserving each source's context.","explain":"Evidence A supports “Land, labor, capital, and entrepreneurship shape production choices.” Evidence B supports “Scarcity requires choices, while incentives affect how households, firms, and governments respond.” Together, the sources show related developments while preserving each source's context. This response states what each source supports and avoids turning related evidence into proof of a single, universal cause.","audio":"Evidence A: A region has mineral resources but lacks roads, trained workers, and investment. Evidence B: A water shortage leads to higher prices, use limits, and investment in conservation. Which thesis best evaluates both sources without overstating either one?"}
